ION O2: \"Fused, as done in TorchOpt\" is operationalized along two axes: (a) the functional restructuring of the optimizer step (TorchOpt's functional differentiable Adam in explicit/backprop differentiation mode \u2014 Condition B), and (b) the fused low-level kernel fast path (`fused=True` \u2014 Condition D, a traceability probe only). The main comparison is A vs B.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. CONDITIONS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFixed shared task for all conditions:\n- Model: deterministic tiny MLP, e.g., 2 linear layers (in\u219216\u21921), ~few hundred to few thousand parameters, seeded init (torch.manual_seed fixed).\n- Data: fixed synthetic batch (e.g., 32 points, seeded), fixed loss (MSE).\n- Inner-loop schedule: K \u2208 {1, 5, 10} inner Adam steps, lr=1e-2, standard eps/betas.\n- Meta-objective: validation loss computed AFTER the K inner steps; the graph of interest is \u2202(meta-loss)/\u2202(initial params) and \u2202(meta-loss)/\u2202(initial optimizer state). Rendering target: torchviz.make_dot on these gradients.\n- Precondition for comparability: all conditions must produce numerically identical (allclose within float tolerance, documented atol/rtol) parameter trajectories and meta-gradients; otherwise graphs do not describe the same computation and are not comparable. This is a validity gate, checked before any metric is collected.\n\nCondition A \u2014 Reference / \"TorchViz output\" (PRIMARY baseline):\n- Hand-written Adam update using primitive torch ops (add, mul, sqrt, div, bias-correction clamp etc.), fully traceable, one op at a time.\n- Rendered with torchviz.make_dot (optionally show_attrs=True).\n\nCondition B \u2014 TorchOpt functional Adam, explicit (backprop-through-optimizer) differentiation mode:\n- torchopt functional API used so the K-step inner loop is traced into the autograd graph.\n- Rendered with the same torchviz.make_dot call (and, secondarily, TorchOpt's own visualization helper if available) for renderer symmetry.\n\nCondition C \u2014 TorchOpt implicit differentiation mode (CONTROL):\n- Expected to yield a near-empty update graph (implicit differentiation does not unroll the update); included to show that \"smaller graph\" via semantics change is NOT the same claim as \"fusing yields a less complex graph,\" and to keep the interpretation honest.\n\nCondition D \u2014 Fused kernel probe (framed as probe only):\n- Attempt to trace the fused/compiled Adam path (`fused=True` / torchopt.compile equivalents) under autograd.\n- NO RESULT PROMISED EITHER WAY. Two documented outcomes are acceptable: (i) graph exists \u2192 include in metrics table; (ii) graph is empty/opaque or autograd errors \u2192 record exclusion reason verbatim and exclude from the A/B comparison, reporting only that the fused kernel path is not autograd-traceable in the tested configuration. The A/B conclusion must not depend on D.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. GRAPH-COUNT NORMALIZATION RULE (defined BEFORE metrics collection)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nPurpose: TorchOpt may internally use functorch/vmap-style transforms whose bookkeeping (e.g., wrapper/transform nodes, scope frames, stacked-VMap nodes) could inflate its counts independent of real computational complexity. Rule, fixed in advance:\nN1. Canonical node set = autograd Function nodes that execute a concrete aten op or a named user-level op (identified by node name), PLUS leaf/parameter and AccumulateGrad nodes (counted once per graph, reported separately in a \"leaf\" row so they can be subtracted).\nN2. Excluded from |V|/|E|: nodes whose name matches a pre-declared bookkeeping pattern list (to be finalized during feasibility smoke tests by inspecting actual node names in Condition B; the list must be frozen in the analysis code and applied identically to all conditions, including A and C \u2014 a symmetric rule, not a TorchOpt-only filter).\nN3. Every excluded node is logged with its name and count; the metrics table reports BOTH raw and normalized counts so the filter's effect is transparent.\nN4. Edges are counted only between retained nodes (or leaf endpoints); edges through excluded nodes are contracted, not dropped.\nN5. The same normalization is applied to saved-tensor counts: bookkeeping saved tensors (e.g., dummy/scope tensors) are excluded per the same pattern list, with raw counts also reported.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n3. METRICS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplexity (per condition \u00d7 K, on \u2202meta/\u2202\u03b8\u2080 and \u2202meta/\u2202(optimizer state) graphs):\n- |V|, |E| (raw and normalized per \u00a72)\n- Graph depth: longest path in the retained DAG\n- Average degree; number of distinct op types\n- Op-category histogram: primitive elementwise `aten::*` nodes vs high-level/fused single-op nodes\n- Saved-tensor count (torch.autograd.graph saved-tensors-hooks) \u2192 memory proxy\n- Backward wall-clock and profiler op count (torch.profiler), CPU-only, warmup + repeated timing with medians\n\nInterpretability (proxies, since interpretability is partly subjective):\n- Readability ratio: fraction of retained nodes whose labels map to recognizable Adam semantics (explicit name dictionary for A: e.g., \"exp_avg update\", \"bias correction\"; for B, whatever labels TorchOpt emits) vs anonymous/generic autograd node names\n- Traceability depth: for each parameter tensor, minimum number of retained ops linking its initial value to its post-update value\n- Rendered-image rubric: the two PNGs per (condition, K) are scored on a pre-registered 1\u20135 rubric (\"can a reader identify the Adam update rule from the graph?\") with the rubric text fixed in advance; scored blind to condition labels where feasible\n- Qualitative side-by-side figures included in the report\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n4. ANALYSIS PLAN AND CLAIM CRITERION\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n- Report a full table: metric \u00d7 condition {A, B, C, (D if included)} \u00d7 K {1, 5, 10}, both graph targets (\u2202/\u2202\u03b8\u2080, \u2202/\u2202state).\n- CLAIM CRITERION (fixed in advance): the answer to the question is \"yes\" only if, at matched numerical outputs (validity gate passed), Condition B shows reduced |V| and |E| and reduced depth relative to Condition A across all tested K, AND B's interpretability measures (readability ratio and rubric) are equal to or better than A's. Partial improvements (e.g., fewer nodes but worse readability) must be reported as mixed results, not \"yes\".\n- IMPORTANT NEUTRALITY NOTE: The expected empirical outcome is UNKNOWN. The design is symmetric and must be capable of supporting either answer: if B's graph is larger or less readable (plausible \u2014 TorchOpt's functional machinery may add structure), the \"no/mixed\" answer is reported with the same rigor. No outcome is baked into the metrics, normalization, or reporting template.\n- Statistical treatment: counts are deterministic per seed; report across \u22653 seeds to check robustness of counts to initialization (counts should be seed-invariant given fixed shapes; any variance is itself reported).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n5. CONTROLS AND VALIDITY CHECKS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nC1. Numerical equivalence gate (allclose on parameter trajectories and meta-gradients across A, B, C where C's semantics permit; C may legitimately differ in gradient values \u2014 document separately).\nC2. Identical rendering path for A and B (same make_dot settings, same show_attrs, same Graphviz version) so renderer differences cannot drive metric differences.\nC3. Fixed seeds; fixed shapes; fixed K; fixed dtype (float32).\nC4. Normalization rule applied symmetrically (\u00a72, N5).\nC5. Determinism check: re-running one configuration must reproduce identical digraph node counts (guards against nondeterministic graph construction).\nC6. Sanity: an intentionally no_grad'd step must render an empty graph \u2014 confirms the tooling detects graph-emptiness (validates Assumption O1's premise).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n6. FEASIBILITY SECTION (all execution-time; not yet done)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nF1. Environment: create a versioned environment (venv or conda with Python \u22643.12, e.g., 3.11) \u2014 because system Python 3.14.4 likely has no prebuilt torch/torchopt wheels (RISK). Fallback plan: (a) try pip wheels for torch+torchopt+torchviz on the host interpreter; (b) if unavailable, install a 3.11/3.12 interpreter via the system package manager or a local env tool; (c) last resort, run with torch only and emulate B with a functional-API stub \u2014 this last resort would be flagged as invalid for the main claim and would require stopping for researcher guidance. Network is currently disabled; installation must occur in the prepared execution environment.\nF2. Version resolution: pin exact versions of torch, torchopt, torchviz, graphviz in a requirements file at install time and record them in the report (offline inspection could not verify current API names/signatures).\nF3. Smoke test 1 (Condition A traceability): tiny script renders make_dot for the hand-written Adam K=1 meta-graph; PASS requires a non-empty digraph with \u22651 non-leaf aten node.\nF4. Smoke test 2 (Condition B traceability): same for torchopt functional Adam in explicit mode; PASS identical criterion. Also record node-name inventory from this run to freeze the \u00a72 bookkeeping pattern list.\nF5. Smoke test 3 (probe D): attempt fused path under autograd; record outcome (traceable / empty / error) verbatim; no exclusion or inclusion decision is made until this is observed.\nF6. Smoke test 4 (C6 above): no_grad step renders empty \u2014 tooling sanity check.\nF7. Compute: trivial (CPU-only, tiny model); total runtime far below any budget concern; actor-step budget of 60 is ample for the script suite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n7. RISKS / LIMITATIONS TO STATE IN THE REPORT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nR1. Interpretability proxies are imperfect; rubric is single-rater unless the researcher supplies additional raters.\nR2. Node-name-based op classification may mislabel some nodes; the classification dictionary is frozen in code and auditable.\nR3. Results are specific to the tested versions and the tested K/shape regime; scaling behavior (bigger models, more steps) is out of scope unless requested.\nR4. Condition C's near-empty graph must not be cited as evidence for the claim (different semantics, not fusing).\nR5. If D is excluded, the word \"fused\" in the final answer is explicitly reinterpreted as the functional restructuring (Assumption O2a), and this reinterpretation is stated in the conclusion.\n\nDeliverable structure when authorized to execute/complete: methods (conditions, task, normalization), metrics definitions, validity checks, feasibility log, results tables (raw + normalized), rendered figures, rubric scores, conclusion following \u00a74 criterion, limitations. Again: design-only at this point \u2014 nothing above has been run, and all figures/tables are placeholders pending execution approval.", "evidence": "Repository observations grounding the design: /workspace/task.json contains the question verbatim and the note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"; /workspace has no other files (no track.md on disk, no source code), so TorchOpt/torchviz facts rest on public documentation and are version-qualified in \u00